# NestedText v3 - DocBook per-version DETAIL pass (measured 2026-09-13)
# Source: cc-resources/.dtd-file-examples/docbook/ - six versions side by side
# Extends mathml-docbook-xhtml11.nt, which gave DocBook one line.

the-trajectory-measured:
    4.2: dtd
    4.3: dtd
    4.4: dtd
    4.5: dtd
    5.0: ChangeLog README VERSION catalog.xml docbook.nvdl dtd rng sch tools xsd
    5.1: catalog.xml docbook.nvdl rng sch tools
    reading:
        > Four versions of DTD alone. Then 5.0 ships EVERY grammar at once, DTD and
        > RELAX NG and Schematron and XSD and an NVDL dispatch. Then 5.1 drops the
        > DTD entirely. The transition version carries both worlds so a consumer can
        > move at its own pace; the version after it does not.
        > This is the clearest measured statement in the whole corpus about what a
        > DTD is for and where it stops.
what-a-dtd-could-not-do:
    > DocBook did not abandon DTD for fashion. It abandoned it for what a DTD cannot
    > express: co-occurrence constraints, context-dependent content, datatypes on
    > text. Schematron covers the first two by asserting rules over a parsed tree;
    > RELAX NG covers the third. NVDL dispatches a document to whichever schema owns
    > each section.
    > The suite's answer to the same limit is already built and named differently:
    > the checkers. dtd/ declares what can be declared, and checker/*.mjs asserts
    > what cannot. mathml-docbook-xhtml11.nt called this layering controls BEHIND
    > the grammar; the version table is the evidence that the layering is forced,
    > not stylistic.
what-this-teaches-the-release:
    the-honest-ceiling:
        > Stream 19 converges the whole DTD family into every subset. DocBook 5.1 is
        > the proof that convergence has a ceiling: past a certain expressiveness the
        > answer is not a better DTD, it is a second instrument. The release should
        > say where that line falls for us rather than discover it at 11.0.0.
    the-transition-version:
        > 5.0 shipping both is the pattern for stream 14. Re-inline at publish AND
        > stamp for the future is exactly a transition version: the current cut and
        > the means to detect a stale one, carried together, for one release.
    nvdl-is-in-our-variant-chain:
        > NVDL sits at depth 10 of the 23-level ladder in 10.0.0.md and DocBook 5.0
        > and 5.1 both ship a docbook.nvdl. The ladder is not a list of formats the
        > release admires; at least one rung is a working dispatch mechanism in a
        > corpus on this disk.
gaps-remaining:
    > The 4.5 DTD content models and the 5.1 Schematron rule set are not
    > transmigrated. The version trajectory, the reason for it and the mapping onto
    > our own checker layering are what streams 14 and 19 needed.
    > MathML and XHTML11 keep the coverage mathml-docbook-xhtml11.nt already gave
    > them; neither needed a second pass for this release.
